WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:10.820
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much for that, may we then move on to Jane, your paper on Safeguarding

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:16.660
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: This is an opportunity to
update the Board on the work of the Safeguarding

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:21.949
team across the NHS, particularly led by NHS
England, and just to point out: we do have

00:00:21.949 --> 00:00:27.669
statutory responsibility for safeguarding.
Sometimes what is not understood is the range

00:00:27.669 --> 00:00:32.450
of work that that encompasses, and so the
paper talks us through the work we have done

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:38.250
around Prevent, child sexual exploitation,
FGM, looked-after children, the Mental Capacity

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:43.960
Act, as well as looking at our child protection
information system and the national independent

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:50.610
enquiry into child sexual abuse, which is
a significant range and quite a breadth of

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:55.230
work. I just thought it would be helpful to
take us through very briefly some of those

00:00:55.230 --> 00:01:01.520
key areas, and obviously, I am very happy
to take questions if that would be useful.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:08.050
The Prevent statutory duty came in place in
2015, and it applies specifically at the moment

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:13.270
to NHS trusts, but we are beginning to do
more and more work now into the commissioning

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:20.090
system. We put some words into our standard
contract, and we have done quite a significant

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:25.530
amount of work with the Home Office more recently
around the fact that there is a reasonable

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:30.550
percentage, something around 30% to 40%, of
some of the perpetrators who actually do have

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:34.690
a mental health problem, and so therefore,
we have needed to work very carefully with

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:39.820
mental health trusts to make sure we have
the right response and the right support,

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:44.630
and we have further guidance being developed,
which will be published in the near future.

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:51.050
The Home Office are satisfied with what we
have been doing. We have some of the highest

00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:55.300
Prevent awareness training out of any of the
public sector, both for the basic one and

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:59.750
the WRAP, which is the more detailed one,
but obviously, it is something that we need

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:07.590
to remain alert and vigilant to, because it
is a potentially significant issue.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:13.269
Around child sexual exploitation, clearly
the Rotherham enquiry really brought that

00:02:13.269 --> 00:02:19.840
into focus, and since then we had done some
significant work with organisations really

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:25.670
around how to recognise it, how to look for
it and, again, working in close participation

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:31.410
with the Home Office, so real cross-government
working around that. The report that they

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:36.640
published actually congratulated us on the
work we have done, and particularly the work

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:40.570
around the child information-sharing system,
which I will talk about in a moment. What

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:43.549
has been done here that is quite important
as well is we have commissioned research from

00:02:43.549 --> 00:02:47.920
Salford University, and one of our acute trusts,
just to make sure that the information we

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:53.069
have given and the guidance we have put out
is well received. The pocket guides that we

00:02:53.069 --> 00:02:56.930
produce for professionals have been flying
out on the shelves, because people actually

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:00.620
want to have it; they want to have it with
them. Very short, very handy for them to look

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:01.620
at.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:10.469
If I move on to FGM very quickly, there is
now mandatory reporting from April 2015, and

00:03:10.469 --> 00:03:18.629
I think the volume has been quite a surprise
to many people. We have over 1,200 newly recorded

00:03:18.629 --> 00:03:25.750
women and children identified every quarter,
and that is a significant number. The vast

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:33.329
majority are London-based, so we have 45%
of new cases in London, and about 44% of the

00:03:33.329 --> 00:03:38.060
total are based in London. I think what we
have done here is both spent a significant

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:44.180
amount of time looking at raising awareness,
but also providing support to support women

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:49.860
and children, in many cases. We have also
done some prevention work, so for example,

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:56.359
before things like the long school holiday
in summer, really working with DfE and others

00:03:56.359 --> 00:04:02.209
to raise awareness in schools, to try and
prevent - literally - some of this with young

00:04:02.209 --> 00:04:06.090
girls going abroad.

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:12.540
The Mental Capacity Act I wanted to just highlight,
because it says in the paper that the Law

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:17.340
Commission was about to publish their report,
and they actually now have done it; they published

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:23.110
it a couple of weeks ago. They have outlined
their recommendations. They are being considered

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:25.850
at the moment by the Department of Health,
and we will need to look at those quite carefully

00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:31.780
because it is possible that that will put
quite significant responsibility around deprivation

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:37.691
of liberty processes with CCGs and the NHS,
so that could have quite a capacity issue

00:04:37.691 --> 00:04:41.190
for us that we need to look at. So, we are
working really closely with the Department

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:47.990
of Health around that, to make sure that we
can monitor and support it as well as we can.

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:52.991
I want to move on to another area that I think
is worth highlighting, and that is the Child

00:04:52.991 --> 00:04:57.710
Protection Information System. The Board will
have had some updates around that in terms

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:03.000
of our corporate performance reports. I think
it is worth pointing out: this is the first

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:08.300
national system of its kind in the world that
actually connects child safety information

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:16.300
between local government and the NHS. I think
it is fair to say we had a slow start. We

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:21.960
are now beginning to roll out quite rapidly,
and since the paper was written, we have moved

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:29.600
from 44 local authorities to 48 that are actually
using CPIS, and we have, in total, something

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:36.020
like 121 NHS settings that are using it as
well. The real benefit of that is: wherever

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:40.740
you are, if you are in an emergency or an
urgent care setting, or you are in social

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:48.710
care, you can actually see that a child is
on an at-risk register, what their plan is,

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:54.000
and social workers can be notified of children
attending. There have been many, many reports

00:05:54.000 --> 00:06:00.230
into child abuse in the past where families
may move around, people may move around different

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:04.500
organisations, and if you actually bring that
information together, warning signs would

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:08.441
have come out, and that has been missed. We
feel, those of us who are involved in this

00:06:08.441 --> 00:06:14.480
area, and as an ex-emergency care nurse myself,
knowing the impact that this can have is significant,

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:17.341
and I am really pleased that we are making
progress on that.

00:06:17.341 --> 00:06:23.340
The last couple of things I just wanted to
highlighted were, first, the modern slavery

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:30.700
work. This, again, is beginning to raise profile.
We have published the first slavery and human

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:35.650
trafficking statement that the Board approved.
We are working really closely with the Anti-Slavery

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:42.830
Commissioner, and we had a very strong role
in the launch of their annual report a few

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:49.080
months ago which, again, we were complimented
on the work that we had done around that.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:54.560
Then last but not least, the safeguarding
reforms. Last year, Alan Wood took a fundamental

00:06:54.560 --> 00:07:03.490
review of the role and function of local safeguarding
children's boards. Again, he made many recommendations.

00:07:03.490 --> 00:07:07.780
We think that the Children and Social Work
Bill is now in the House of Commons awaiting

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:13.380
royal assent; that is going to mean that we
need to have new statutory guidance and new

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:20.880
changes from April 2018. Again, that is likely
to put some significant emphasis on CCGs and

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:27.550
the NHS to do more to support, so again, we
will bring back to you any concerns or updates

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:28.950
that we have from that.

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:34.860
The priorities are effectively more of the
same. The only difference is, we have highlighted

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:41.250
here some more work around domestic abuse
as being important as we move into 2017/2018.

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:46.961
Our Annual Safeguarding Update will be published
next month, and we also have our fourth National

00:07:46.961 --> 00:07:51.390
Safeguarding Conference on 21st April; if
any of the non-executives or directors would

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:53.380
like to come to that, you would be very welcome.

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:59.380
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Jane, for what
I have to say I found a surprisingly wide-ranging

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:04.470
report; I had not appreciated the array of
activity that came under the safeguarding

00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:10.160
umbrella, so thank you for the summary.

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:20.640
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Thanks, Jane, for that very
useful update. On the IT system, it is encouraging

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:26.030
that the uptake is continuing. It frequently
appeared as red on the risk register; does

00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:28.490
that mean it is no longer red, in your terms?

00:08:28.490 --> 00:08:33.329
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: It is certainly moving away
from that. What we have done is we have worked

00:08:33.329 --> 00:08:39.749
really closely with NHS Digital. We did a
review, we re-based it and we have actually

00:08:39.749 --> 00:08:45.880
had some new approach to how it is being implemented,
and it is working. The team have worked incredibly

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:51.060
hard to move this, because it is such a priority,
but I think we can definitely see things moving

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:55.600
and the team feel much more confident now
about the roll-out.

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:59.670
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: A couple more points, if I may?
I was surprised to see the modern slavery

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:07.861
within care settings, the special training
that has been around care settings. Could

00:09:07.861 --> 00:09:17.720
you say a little bit more about that? Obviously,
modern slavery takes a number of forms, but

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:21.750
has there been a particular identification
in the care setting?

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:26.220
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: There has been some indication
of some issues within care settings, which

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:30.000
is why the team have been prompted to do some
more work with them, but I can give you some

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.149
more details of that offline, if that would
be useful?

00:09:34.149 --> 00:09:37.779
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Thank you. A question about
whether there is any training that the Board

00:09:37.779 --> 00:09:43.160
should undertake, certainly in relation to
Prevent? I know that other boards have, that

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:45.699
I have sat on.

00:09:45.699 --> 00:09:54.170
Finally, just to comment on the looked-after
children. Paragraph 24 talks about looked-after

00:09:54.170 --> 00:09:58.370
children under the care of local authorities
having poorer health outcomes, which is of

00:09:58.370 --> 00:10:04.499
course true, but it depends what you mean
by their peers. If they are compared to the

00:10:04.499 --> 00:10:10.250
whole cohort of that age group, of course,
that is true, but if you compare them to children

00:10:10.250 --> 00:10:12.990
from similar circumstances, they have better
outcomes.

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:22.759
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: I think that is against the
rest of the children, so it is back to Victor's

00:10:22.759 --> 00:10:24.449
point about the inverse care law.

00:10:24.449 --> 00:10:25.592
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: It is not the care system?

00:10:25.592 --> 00:10:27.480
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: No, it is not about the care
system. It is not a criticism of the care

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:32.500
system; it is a criticism of the fact that
we have a wide variation, which is why we

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:37.111
are tackling it through Leading Change, Adding
Value, and the Right Care system, the Right

00:10:37.111 --> 00:10:41.290
Care team, which is around how to reduce variation
across the board.

00:10:41.290 --> 00:10:45.420
In terms of your suggestion about training,
one of the questions in the Board paper is:

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:53.339
would the Board like either any information
or any update? We could very happily provide

00:10:53.339 --> 00:10:59.350
some training around both safeguarding more
generally, but specifically around some of

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:02.459
the areas that I have highlighted in the paper.

00:11:02.459 --> 00:11:09.620
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Yes, the care system is
a very good example of the inverse care law

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:18.209
in action. Are there any barriers to rapidly
increasing the roll-out of the Child Protection

00:11:18.209 --> 00:11:23.779
Information System? Because clearly, the slower
it goes, the more there is a huge risk to

00:11:23.779 --> 00:11:31.559
children who are not on it. I am just wondering
how we might work with the system of both

00:11:31.559 --> 00:11:37.009
health and local government to remove barriers.
It seems to me that it is one of those things

00:11:37.009 --> 00:11:43.569
that we ought to be getting regular readings
on, because it is mission critical, really,

00:11:43.569 --> 00:11:45.420
talking about children.

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:51.420
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Yes. We did have - and probably
still to some extent have - some barriers,

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:59.579
or maybe just constraints, I think. There
were some technical issues that included things

00:11:59.579 --> 00:12:04.089
like the roll-out of N3 and a variety of other
things, which took slightly longer to address

00:12:04.089 --> 00:12:12.980
than we had originally anticipated. We have
a different team and really good support for

00:12:12.980 --> 00:12:19.329
NHS Digital now with that. The other issue
has been capacity constraints within local

00:12:19.329 --> 00:12:25.459
authorities, actually being able to put the
time to this and actually deliver what we

00:12:25.459 --> 00:12:30.300
need to do. I think all of those are being
managed, all of them are being supported,

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:35.671
and as we are getting more and more roll-out
and more people are seeing the benefits, the

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:36.671
better it is getting.

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:40.520
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Just one more question.
I agree with everything that Malcolm said

00:12:40.529 --> 00:12:45.339
about the breadth of this report. Do we have
any notion of what the increased capacity

00:12:45.339 --> 00:12:49.180
is that will be required on NHS England?

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:53.690
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Not yet. That is part of
the work the team are doing at the moment.

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:58.330
First of all, we needed to see what the recommendations
were, which have only just been published

00:12:58.330 --> 00:13:05.820
by the Law Commission. The work with the local
child safeguarding boards, the Alan Wood work,

00:13:05.820 --> 00:13:10.149
we have been working directly with the Department
for some time on those, so we will be able

00:13:10.149 --> 00:13:13.740
to come back with some more information on
that.

00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:19.851
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Jane.
We note the report and commend the work. May

00:13:19.851 --> 00:13:24.449
we think further about whether there are other
activities that the Board might get involved

00:13:24.449 --> 00:13:28.999
in, just to make sure that we are on top of
the demands that these obligations place upon

00:13:28.999 --> 00:13:29.999
us as an organisation?

00:13:29.999 --> 00:13:30.499
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Of course you can.

